<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Meskhetian Turks</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Meskhetian Turks are a Turkish-speaking Muslim people originally from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samtskhe">Meskheti</a>, the region of south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhaltsikhe">Akhaltsikhe</a> along the Turkish border. Many descend from the Muslim population of the Ottoman Childir Eyalet. In November 1944 Stalin's government deported them, some 90,000 people, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>; most never returned, and they now live in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazakhstan">Kazakhstan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/meskhetian-turks.jpg" alt="Meskhetian Turks" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ahmet Fehmi Moussa · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ahiska_Turks_in_Karab%C3%BCk,_Turkey.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Meskhetian Turks, also called Ahiska Turks, speak the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kars">Kars</a> dialect of Turkish and belong to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanafi-school">Hanafi school</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni Islam</a>. They historically lived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samtskhe">Meskheti</a>, the region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> along the Turkish border. Turkic tribes settled there as early as the eleventh and twelfth centuries, and the Turkish presence began with the Ottoman expedition of 1578. By the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-amasya">Peace of Amasya</a> in 1555 Meskheti had been divided between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavids</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>, and by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-zuhab">Treaty of Zuhab</a> in 1639 all of it fell under Ottoman control. Most Meskhetian Turks see themselves as descended from Ottoman settlers.</p><p>Pro-Georgian historiography has argued that they are Turkified Meskhetians, a Georgian ethnographic subgroup converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> under Ottoman rule. The anthropologist Anatoly Khazanov has argued that this oversimplifies the matter, because their traditional culture, though it held some Georgian elements, was close to the Turkish one. Soviet documents of 1944 called them simply Turks, and the term Meskhetian came into use only in the late 1950s. Most call themselves Turks or Ahiskan Turks.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>On 15 November 1944 the Soviet government under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-stalin">Joseph Stalin</a> ordered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deportation">deportation</a> of the Meskhetian Turks from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>. They were secretly driven from their homes and loaded onto rail cars, and as many as 30,000 to 50,000 died of hunger, thirst and cold. They had been accused of smuggling, banditry and espionage with their kin across the border. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> was preparing a pressure campaign against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, and in June 1945 Vyacheslav Molotov demanded the cession of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kars">Kars</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardahan">Ardahan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artvin">Artvin</a>. Stalin wanted the strategic border region cleared of people likely to be hostile to Soviet intentions. The deportation was officially acknowledged only in 1968.</p><p>Unlike other deported Muslim groups, the Meskhetians were not rehabilitated or permitted to return. In 1970 the leaders of their national movement asked the Turkish embassy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> to let them emigrate to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, and the Soviet government answered by arresting them. In 1989 riots broke out in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fergana-valley">Fergana Valley</a> between Meskhetian Turks and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbeks">Uzbeks</a>. Hundreds were killed or injured and nearly 1,000 properties were destroyed. About 70,000 went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>, and others to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazakhstan">Kazakhstan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyrgyzstan">Kyrgyzstan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The Soviet census of 1989 counted 207,502 Turks in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, but many Meskhetian Turks had been recorded as Azeri, Kazakh, Kyrgyz or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbeks">Uzbek</a>, so the official figures understate them. They remain dispersed across the former Soviet Union and live also in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. Only a few hundred have been able to return to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Meskhetian Turks” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
